<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="troubleshooting"
      toc={[
        {"runner-connect", "A runner won't connect"},
        {"offline-stuck", "Offline runners and stuck runs"},
        {"failed-refused-denied", "Failed, refused, or denied"},
        {"pack-pending", "A pack is pending or blocking dispatch"},
        {"limits", "Operational limits"},
        {"data-residency", "Where your data lives"},
        {"support", "Still stuck?"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="troubleshooting">
        <:dek>
          Something isn't behaving — a runner won't come online, a run came back <em>denied</em>
          instead of done, or you've bumped a size or rate limit. This page is the triage order:
          what each failure actually means, where the answer is, and the operational caps worth
          knowing before you script against the API.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="runner-connect">A runner won't connect</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner names the cause in its own logs — start there before you touch config.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># what the runner is doing right now</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> journalctl -u emisar -f                   <span class="text-zinc-500"># Linux (systemd)</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> tail -f /var/log/emisar/emisar.err.log    <span class="text-zinc-500"># macOS (launchd)</span>

<span class="text-zinc-500"># service stuck in "failed" after repeated crashes — clear it, then start</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo systemctl reset-failed emisar &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl start emisar</.docs_code>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Egress is blocked.</strong>
            A connect timeout means the host can't reach the control plane. The runner dials
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">outbound 443</strong>
            (a websocket) to your portal —
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar.dev</code>
            by default, set as
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">cloud.url</code>
            in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/config.yaml</code>.
            The installer additionally needs
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">api.github.com</code>
            and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">github.com</code>
            on 443 to download and checksum-verify the signed binary, and
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar pack install</code>
            fetches packs from the registry over the same 443. Open those and retry — the runner
            never accepts inbound connections, so no ingress rule is involved.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The enrollment key was spent or revoked.</strong>
            A <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">401</code>
            on a first connect means the
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emkey-enroll-…</code>
            key was single-use and already spent, or it expired, ran out of uses, or was revoked.
            Mint a fresh one under <strong>Runners → Enrollment keys</strong>
            (or generate a new
            install one-liner) and put it in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/runner.env</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The runner's token was rejected.</strong>
            If the host connected before and now gets a <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">401</code>,
            the runner was deleted in the dashboard — its long-lived
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">rnrtok-…</code>
            token no longer authenticates, so the service exits rather than retrying forever.
            Re-enroll with a fresh key to bring the host back. (Disabling a runner is the reversible
            alternative: the service stays up and keeps retrying until you re-enable it — see <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/runners"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >the runner fleet page</.link>.)
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The service went "failed."</strong>
            After five failed starts within five minutes, systemd stops retrying on purpose — a
            permanently-broken config (a revoked key returning 401) shouldn't hammer the control
            plane. Fix the cause the logs named, then run the
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reset-failed</code>
            command above.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="offline-stuck">Offline runners and stuck runs</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner heartbeats every 30 seconds and the dashboard's status reflects it. A brief
        disconnect is normal: the runner reconnects with exponential backoff, re-advertises its
        catalog, and in-flight actions keep executing through the gap, with results delivered on
        the new connection. Nothing sits "running" forever — a background sweep resolves anything
        that stopped making progress:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Runner offline past a two-minute grace.</strong>
            An in-flight run whose runner stays disconnected past the grace is marked
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">errored</strong>
            with a plain reason ("the result never arrived"), so a run never hangs behind a host
            that vanished.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Accepted but never returned.</strong>
            A dispatch a runner accepted but never produced a durable result for is errored after
            about ten minutes — and <strong class="text-zinc-200">never silently re-run</strong>.
            Emisar executes an accepted action at most once, so an unknown outcome is surfaced for
            you to decide, not retried behind your back.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Queued for a runner that left.</strong>
            A run queued for a runner that went offline, was disabled, or was removed before the
            dispatch reached it is errored with "the action never reached it." Nothing ran, so
            these are safe to re-dispatch once the host is back.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The run's detail page shows the exact reason; the <strong>Runners</strong>
        page shows whether the host is connected. A runner that flaps between connected and offline
        is almost always egress or a wandering host clock — check both.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="failed-refused-denied">Failed, refused, or denied</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        These look alike when you're in a hurry, but only one of them means the command actually
        ran on the host. Read the run's status and error message first — the distinction tells you
        where to look next.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Denied.</strong>
            A policy rejected the dispatch <em>before</em>
            anything executed. The run carries the
            policy's reason; filter <strong>Audit</strong>
            by <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">action_run.denied</code>
            to see it with the actor and target. Fix it by adjusting the policy or routing the
            action through an approval — see <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >policies and approvals</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Refused.</strong>
            The runner itself refused on a pre-execution trust check — a dispatch signature that
            didn't validate, or on-disk pack bytes that didn't match the trusted hash. Nothing ran;
            the specific cause is in the run's error message. For a signature refusal, the reason
            codes and their fixes are in <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch" <> "#troubleshooting"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >signed dispatch → Troubleshooting a refusal</.link>. For a hash mismatch, see the next section.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Rejected before exec.</strong>
            The runner re-validates every argument against the schema it loaded locally: a bad
            value comes back as a validation error, and an action ID the host doesn't advertise
            comes back as unknown. Nothing ran — usually catalog drift (the pack changed or isn't
            installed on that host). Check
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo emisar action list</code>
            on the host to see what it actually advertises.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Failed.</strong>
            The action ran and its command exited non-zero. This is the tool's own failure, not a
            gate: read the exit code and stderr on the run detail, and the host journal
            (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo emisar events tail</code>) for the full masked command.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Timed out.</strong>
            The action exceeded its declared timeout and was cancelled. If the work legitimately
            takes longer, raise the action's timeout in the pack.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The run detail page is the fastest triage — status, exit code, output, policy, and error
        message in one place. <strong>Audit</strong>
        is where you pivot across many runs by actor or event type; how to read and export it is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >audit &amp; SIEM</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="pack-pending">A pack is pending or blocking dispatch</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Packs are administrator-trusted content, pinned by content hash. Two states stop an action
        from dispatching, both by design:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pending.</strong>
            A newly installed pack version — or changed pack bytes after a runner reload — lands as
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">pending</strong>
            on the <strong>Packs</strong>
            page until an admin trusts it. Dispatch to that version is blocked until you review and
            trust it. That's what makes a pack update visible instead of silent.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Drift (hash mismatch).</strong>
            The control plane sends the runner the trusted hash with every dispatch; the runner
            re-hashes the on-disk pack and refuses to run on a mismatch (a
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">refused</strong>
            run). If a host's bytes drifted from what you trusted — a hand-edit, a half-finished
            update — restore the trusted bytes, or, if the change is intended, trust the new version
            on the Packs page.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># loaded packs + their on-disk content hashes on this host</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack list

<span class="text-zinc-500"># re-pin a drifted host back to the trusted bytes</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack update &lt;id&gt;</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Compare the host's hash against the trusted hash shown on the Packs page to confirm drift.
        The full pack model — trust, drift, and versioning — is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >the pack reference</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="limits">Operational limits</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The caps Emisar enforces so one noisy action, one busy agent, or one aggressive export
        can't overwhelm a page or the API. Most are generous; these are the ones worth knowing
        before you build against them.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-900">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/80 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-zinc-500">
              <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">What</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">Cap</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">At the cap</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">Action output</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Set per action in the pack (a few KB to a few MB)
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Bytes past the cap are dropped from the delivered output but counted, and the run is
                flagged truncated.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">
                Live output in the dashboard
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">The most recent 500 progress lines</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Older lines are trimmed from the browser view with a note; the run history and the
                host journal keep the full record.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">MCP list pages</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                15 per page by default (up to 50;
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">recent_runs</code>
                up to 100,
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">find_actions</code>
                up to 15)
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Continue with the cursor the previous response returned; a catalog cursor is bound
                to its exact query and expires after 15 minutes.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">MCP long-poll wait</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">Up to 60 seconds per call</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">run_action</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">wait_for_run</code>, and
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">recent_runs</code>
                return the current state at the deadline; one credential may hold 8 concurrent waits
                per node.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">MCP response size</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">512 KiB per tool result</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Oversized structured output is dropped and replaced with a
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">wait_for_run</code>
                continuation that fetches it.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">Runbooks over MCP</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Up to 32 steps, 16 runners per step, 256 runs, and a 56 KiB definition
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Larger runbooks still run from the dashboard; only the model-facing projection is
                bounded.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">Audit export</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">100 events per page, 1,000 maximum</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Cursor-paginated forward; page with the cursor each response returns.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-medium text-zinc-200">Audit retention</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                7 days on Free, 90 on Team, 365 on Enterprise
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                Fixed per event when it's written; stream to your SIEM to keep it longer.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="Scripting against the API?">
        The full MCP contract — every method, parameter, error code, and the idempotency rules that
        make a retried dispatch safe — is in the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >MCP reference</.link>.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="data-residency">Where your data lives</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner, its packs, and its local journal live on your hosts. Emisar's control plane —
        the dashboard, the audit log, run history, and the MCP API — is a hosted service on <strong class="text-zinc-200">Google Cloud in the United States</strong>. Data is encrypted
        at rest with provider-managed keys and in transit with TLS, and the runner only ever dials
        out, so nothing reaches back into your hosts. For the full posture — subprocessors, backups,
        the runner trust boundary, and how to verify a release yourself — see the
        <.link href={~p"/security"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">security overview</.link>
        and the <.link href={~p"/trust"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">trust page</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="support">Still stuck?</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When the runner's logs and the run's error message don't explain it, grab the run id (it's
        in the run's URL) and the host's service logs around that time, and send them to <a
          href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.support()}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >support@emisar.dev</a>.
        That pair is almost always enough to find the cause quickly.
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># service logs around the failure, ready to attach to a support email</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> journalctl -u emisar --since <span class="text-brand-300">"10 minutes ago"</span></.docs_code>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
